{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MdroAFiqwVQToATxatXffF","type":"tool_result","content":"   358→## 9. Key Files Reference\n   359→\n   360→| File                                   | Purpose                                                                       |\n   361→| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   362→| `server/index.ts`                      | CLI entry point — arg parsing, routing to commands                            |\n   363→| `server/hankweave-runtime.ts`          | Core runtime — codon execution, state management, event emission              |\n   364→| `server/config.ts`                     | 5-layer config resolution (defaults → hankweave.json → hank.json → env → CLI) |\n   365→| `server/basic-tui.ts`                  | Built-in terminal UI (WebSocket client)                                       |\n   366→| `server/codon-runner.ts`               | Runs individual codons via harness managers                                   |\n   367→| `server/claude-agent-sdk-manager.ts`   | Claude Code SDK integration                                                   |\n   368→| `server/shim-process-manager.ts`       | Process manager for shim-based harnesses (Codex, Gemini)                      |\n   369→| `server/remote-hank.ts`                | Git-based remote hank fetching and caching                                    |\n   370→| `server/sentinels/`                    | Sentinel system (event-triggered observers)                                   |\n   371→| `server/telemetry/`                    | Anonymous usage telemetry (PostHog)                                           |\n   372→| `server/schemas/event-schemas.ts`      | WebSocket event type definitions (Zod)                                        |\n   373→| `server/config-validation/`            | Validation schemas for hank files and configs                                 |\n   374→| `tests/utils/sentinel-test-harness.ts` | Shared test utilities for sentinel tests                                      |\n   375→| `tests/utils/mock-llm.ts`              | Mock LLM provider for integration tests                                       |\n   376→| `docs/package.json`                    | Documentation site workspace (hankweave-docs)                                 |\n   377→| `docs/content/`                        | MDX documentation pages (concepts, guides, reference)                         |\n   378→\n   379→---\n   380→\n   381→## 10. Environment & Secrets\n   382→\n   383→### Local development\n   384→\n   385→No secrets needed for unit/integration tests. E2E tests need:\n   386→\n   387→- `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` — Claude Code access\n   388→- `GEMINI_API_KEY` — Gemini CLI access\n   389→- `HANKWEAVE_SENTINEL_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — for sentinel LLM calls\n   390→- Codex auth via `~/.codex/auth.json`\n   391→\n   392→### CI secrets\n   393→\n   394→Same as above, plus:\n   395→\n   396→- `NPM_TOKEN` — for npm publish\n   397→- `CODEX_AUTH_JSON` — Codex authentication JSON\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43c754e8-4bc1-4bd6-9886-b45962e4c4e6","uuid":"7b38b132-25fe-41ae-971e-74b66950eb9c","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md","content":"## 9. Key Files Reference\n\n| File                                   | Purpose                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `server/index.ts`                      | CLI entry point — arg parsing, routing to commands                            |\n| `server/hankweave-runtime.ts`          | Core runtime — codon execution, state management, event emission              |\n| `server/config.ts`                     | 5-layer config resolution (defaults → hankweave.json → hank.json → env → CLI) |\n| `server/basic-tui.ts`                  | Built-in terminal UI (WebSocket client)                                       |\n| `server/codon-runner.ts`               | Runs individual codons via harness managers                                   |\n| `server/claude-agent-sdk-manager.ts`   | Claude Code SDK integration                                                   |\n| `server/shim-process-manager.ts`       | Process manager for shim-based harnesses (Codex, Gemini)                      |\n| `server/remote-hank.ts`                | Git-based remote hank fetching and caching                                    |\n| `server/sentinels/`                    | Sentinel system (event-triggered observers)                                   |\n| `server/telemetry/`                    | Anonymous usage telemetry (PostHog)                                           |\n| `server/schemas/event-schemas.ts`      | WebSocket event type definitions (Zod)                                        |\n| `server/config-validation/`            | Validation schemas for hank files and configs                                 |\n| `tests/utils/sentinel-test-harness.ts` | Shared test utilities for sentinel tests                                      |\n| `tests/utils/mock-llm.ts`              | Mock LLM provider for integration tests                                       |\n| `docs/package.json`                    | Documentation site workspace (hankweave-docs)                                 |\n| `docs/content/`                        | MDX documentation pages (concepts, guides, reference)                         |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Environment & Secrets\n\n### Local development\n\nNo secrets needed for unit/integration tests. E2E tests need:\n\n- `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` — Claude Code access\n- `GEMINI_API_KEY` — Gemini CLI access\n- `HANKWEAVE_SENTINEL_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — for sentinel LLM calls\n- Codex auth via `~/.codex/auth.json`\n\n### CI secrets\n\nSame as above, plus:\n\n- `NPM_TOKEN` — for npm publish\n- `CODEX_AUTH_JSON` — Codex authentication JSON","numLines":40,"startLine":358,"totalLines":429}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T14:01:58.379Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PXy4b2AcduTpaTs5hpCAmb","type":"tool_result","content":"    74→## 3. Development Workflow\n    75→\n    76→### The four-eyes principle\n    77→\n    78→We operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n    79→\n    80→1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n    81→2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n    82→3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n    83→4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n    84→5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n    85→6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n    86→7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n    87→8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n    88→\n    89→**Less code is more.** Hankweave has concurrent components and is deployed across multiple environments. Every line should be considered for its implications. Get as much reviewed locally before opening the PR.\n    90→\n    91→ALL changes have at least two pairs of eyes reviewing them, and have enough deliberation. Often a review is requested from whoever is the implicit owner or originator of a section.\n    92→\n    93→### Intermediates\n    94→\n    95→The `intermediates/` directory holds planning documents for features and investigations. Numbered sequentially (not tied to Linear tickets). Structure varies:\n    96→\n    97→- Single file: `intermediates/7-cleanup-integration-improvements.md`\n    98→- Multi-phase: `intermediates/12-execution-isolation/1-plan.md` → `2-test-fixes.md` → `3-start-new.md` → `4-completed.md`\n    99→- Bug reports: `intermediates/39-QoL-3/BUG_REPORT_HEADLESS_HTTP_CRASH.md`\n   100→\n   101→These are stripped from release/alpha and the public repo.\n   102→\n   103→### External docs\n   104→\n   105→`external-docs/` holds SDK documentation and reference material (Claude Code SDK, AI SDK, Gemini CLI, etc.). These exist primarily so AI agents have context when working on integrations. Stripped before release.\n   106→\n   107→### Documentation site\n   108→\n   109→The documentation site is a Bun workspace at `docs/`. To work on it locally, first ensure dependencies are installed from the repo root (`bun install`), then:\n   110→\n   111→    cd docs && bun run dev    # Development server with hot reload (uses --turbopack)\n   112→    cd docs && bun run build  # Production build\n   113→\n   114→Changes to docs pages (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`) deploy automatically via Vercel when pushed to the configured branch.\n   115→\n   116→Schema generation writes to both `schemas/` (for the npm package) and `docs/public/schemas/` (for the docs site):\n   117→\n   118→    bun run generate-schemas\n   119→\n   120→---\n   121→\n   122→## 4. PR Conventions\n   123→\n   124→### Title format\n   125→\n   126→Include Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n   127→\n   128→```\n   129→Fixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\n   130→Quality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\n   131→CC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n   132→```\n   133→\n   134→Linear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n   135→\n   136→### Body format\n   137→\n   138→PR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n   139→\n   140→- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n   141→- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n   142→- **Files** — which files were touched\n   143→\n   144→Batched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n   145→\n   146→### Changelog entries\n   147→\n   148→Changelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n   149→\n   150→```markdown\n   151→- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n   152→```\n   153→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43c754e8-4bc1-4bd6-9886-b45962e4c4e6","uuid":"dbf91422-3c25-433b-b5f8-a7be6622d7f9","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md","content":"## 3. Development Workflow\n\n### The four-eyes principle\n\nWe operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n\n1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n\n**Less code is more.** Hankweave has concurrent components and is deployed across multiple environments. Every line should be considered for its implications. Get as much reviewed locally before opening the PR.\n\nALL changes have at least two pairs of eyes reviewing them, and have enough deliberation. Often a review is requested from whoever is the implicit owner or originator of a section.\n\n### Intermediates\n\nThe `intermediates/` directory holds planning documents for features and investigations. Numbered sequentially (not tied to Linear tickets). Structure varies:\n\n- Single file: `intermediates/7-cleanup-integration-improvements.md`\n- Multi-phase: `intermediates/12-execution-isolation/1-plan.md` → `2-test-fixes.md` → `3-start-new.md` → `4-completed.md`\n- Bug reports: `intermediates/39-QoL-3/BUG_REPORT_HEADLESS_HTTP_CRASH.md`\n\nThese are stripped from release/alpha and the public repo.\n\n### External docs\n\n`external-docs/` holds SDK documentation and reference material (Claude Code SDK, AI SDK, Gemini CLI, etc.). These exist primarily so AI agents have context when working on integrations. Stripped before release.\n\n### Documentation site\n\nThe documentation site is a Bun workspace at `docs/`. To work on it locally, first ensure dependencies are installed from the repo root (`bun install`), then:\n\n    cd docs && bun run dev    # Development server with hot reload (uses --turbopack)\n    cd docs && bun run build  # Production build\n\nChanges to docs pages (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`) deploy automatically via Vercel when pushed to the configured branch.\n\nSchema generation writes to both `schemas/` (for the npm package) and `docs/public/schemas/` (for the docs site):\n\n    bun run generate-schemas\n\n---\n\n## 4. PR Conventions\n\n### Title format\n\nInclude Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n\n```\nFixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\nQuality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\nCC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n```\n\nLinear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n\n### Body format\n\nPR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n\n- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n- **Files** — which files were touched\n\nBatched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n\n### Changelog entries\n\nChangelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n\n```markdown\n- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n```\n","numLines":80,"startLine":74,"totalLines":429}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T14:01:58.404Z"}
